#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "dfba5156-6b5f-5622-9406-7598ae2630b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12449" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560128 :question-generator/id #uuid "3600eb1c-4459-58b3-921a-d4624ad93397" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186133931.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfbacc2c-3295-5427-aa6d-abb9d46ca58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130991} {:answer/id #uuid "068f8b0d-155c-5288-ab99-7bef355ce630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130992} {:answer/id #uuid "217bf025-09a6-5388-b75e-9b4aa6c0931a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130993} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8b540c-c595-5796-b0a9-c06e4f0ff6a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130994} {:answer/id #uuid "4935e6a8-5d04-5659-bd19-c0da643e24ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "068f8b0d-155c-5288-ab99-7bef355ce630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130992} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf5d99a-43d2-5f4d-9cab-2d45271876ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4d6f3d3-e8a0-54e3-96bf-06ac782d45a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162412" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=2xz\\,i+({x}^{2}+z)\\,j+2\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2) \\\\  & =2z+0+0 \\\\  & =2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(1-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "305d1aaf-bbea-5c5f-a549-96aeb913edda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279014} {:answer/id #uuid "7052a4ed-9911-5fd7-bc04-95f5aa7e4565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279610} {:answer/id #uuid "774d71e4-5b4e-524f-b577-edc0dd3ee10b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279657} {:answer/id #uuid "ca898aae-6598-59af-88de-b8842924f741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280367} {:answer/id #uuid "cd52ceb4-0135-536f-8588-e294bafcb89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd52ceb4-0135-536f-8588-e294bafcb89b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280401} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6048b0a-8782-5f08-a33d-6088d1e77789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b54a2292-f496-593a-8f3d-c0762d77f082" :question/math-academy-id "q-162606" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,j+(x+y)\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+0+0 \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "638bdd3c-988f-5b06-9b5e-88c81e7acaff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "638bdd3c-988f-5b06-9b5e-88c81e7acaff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234699} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9aaca52-991c-54fa-a8a1-2c32329e23f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dc47c56-52c7-5494-aceb-b1baa61e89b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-168617" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nIn part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082e3c86-e949-5ff4-b33f-cb02c016d1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278667} {:answer/id #uuid "149426fb-57b1-536b-9c11-dd047080a433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278775} {:answer/id #uuid "16d8f309-fda3-575c-a9d3-a8ec0f44cdcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278793} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae65590-f27f-55dc-9769-e7944f343866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279261} {:answer/id #uuid "9c741780-eba9-5519-8797-113033813b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c741780-eba9-5519-8797-113033813b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279984} :answer-field/id #uuid "af809870-df22-50bf-a671-64310449b9b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e758b54-bd1f-5329-88cb-61523affaf73" :question/math-academy-id "q-162594" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=4,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}y\\,i+2z\\,j+xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz) \\\\  & =2xy+0+xy \\\\  & =3xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}3\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4}\\cdot [3z]_{z=1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (8-0)\\cdot (6-3) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52186257-9f4b-56fb-b960-29a2f63ea399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279339} {:answer/id #uuid "697dee97-f93f-5355-b963-f4f2ed4536b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279547} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c9b24c-57c8-5afa-a32e-082d761b5954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280164} {:answer/id #uuid "e0aaa464-a730-5cbb-ab6f-fdbf65421878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280578} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf8c058-83e0-502b-802f-07d67539564b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "697dee97-f93f-5355-b963-f4f2ed4536b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279547} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ac0596-1f70-519e-8661-fc2d524e2e33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dea24fde-a211-5010-b92d-b13afeca9f75" :question/math-academy-id "q-162596" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=e,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(x-{y}^{3})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x=1}^{x=e}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot [z]_{z=0}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2})\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453e572a-9b7a-5c8b-af0a-d4bbab3e02c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279211} {:answer/id #uuid "719c2ade-ec2a-5fc4-81bc-42bee8f852aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279622} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b4c658-0c6e-58c5-a593-f19bdd138a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280083} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e4f34b-b03f-5102-8a05-51e7755ec9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280208} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7e88cc-1b76-537b-a6f1-169efb2d510e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8b4c658-0c6e-58c5-a593-f19bdd138a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280083} :answer-field/id #uuid "e81d88b2-55dd-5e26-8ccb-24f90e955d12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7cc5e4b-aa70-5d55-82a4-140961e3cc36" :question/math-academy-id "q-162599" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+(x+{y}^{2})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x+2+0 \\\\  & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+2x]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+2(1)-0)\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0828f14c-57b5-5bff-96a4-41f2ecf9c8cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278666} {:answer/id #uuid "601888a6-bae8-5265-8032-ca7466880974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279474} {:answer/id #uuid "6f899f87-fc79-53d1-9d8b-59f00b8892d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279603} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7b1ce6-5db6-5b72-8254-8b233c23921a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280411} {:answer/id #uuid "f34738d0-7faa-5814-82e1-6d902521196c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f899f87-fc79-53d1-9d8b-59f00b8892d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279603} :answer-field/id #uuid "22288074-59ca-5f02-9943-023604b9357b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a55db798-fcb0-54c3-9d76-3d7d1e841ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-162600" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=2y\\,i+x{y}^{2}\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+2xy+2xy \\\\  & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25469fa9-aa9b-5ce2-912f-092bcf0c9271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374202} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee396c4-83e0-5fa2-ae23-69048563ecb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374203} {:answer/id #uuid "ddac6d93-fada-5b42-9d98-dffa0367488c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374204} {:answer/id #uuid "c8406793-5096-5aff-b557-a3bebc57cf38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374205} {:answer/id #uuid "88de07a7-1a0f-5f7b-b1a1-718b8c6821c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddac6d93-fada-5b42-9d98-dffa0367488c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374204} :answer-field/id #uuid "940992af-dba8-51a2-a024-f36c67d58c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1604058-cdd6-524d-a2be-ab81c147d2ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-162597" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=3,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(y-{x}^{2})\\,i+xy\\,j+3xz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3xz) \\\\  & =(-2x)+x+3x \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=1}^{x=3}\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(9-1)\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1a9148-8e77-5639-ad7f-cbca7f0df0cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374209} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcc0510-4039-55e0-823c-e4ac7d5653d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374210} {:answer/id #uuid "0acfab67-9718-5e64-b967-a1914c71eddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374211} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf62725-3d23-5464-bf59-4f18a980c4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374212} {:answer/id #uuid "702faaa4-c282-5398-860f-c6e1f3c9bb1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1a9148-8e77-5639-ad7f-cbca7f0df0cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374209} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ced5422-0477-54ea-a063-de1490b4e5d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2a8924c-323c-54ea-b830-8522b9835da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162602" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=3,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(2{x}^{3}+z)\\,i-z\\,j-3{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-3{x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+0+(-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=-1}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3})\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f5be7e-0bfc-59a7-af3a-6dfcf5d7d844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430913} {:answer/id #uuid "23e226ce-01b7-5120-bfc5-5949d300bfcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430916} {:answer/id #uuid "475b50f4-a66e-582c-bb09-b18513917dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430921} {:answer/id #uuid "e03f06d7-09f6-5991-8022-a31e044488bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430935} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430938} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc27d6bf-860e-505c-82f5-df4b985c7b2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09c354f4-ffe0-5d80-b23d-97b7671b8b06" :question/math-academy-id "q-162595" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y{z}^{2}\\,i-2yz\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2z)+6z \\\\  & =4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [2{z}^{2}]_{z=-1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (8-2) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ea0e2b-5e7a-52c9-a1b3-f61827c82bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430917} {:answer/id #uuid "676e7034-5bd6-59ee-bc64-fafa0a12a224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430926} {:answer/id #uuid "727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430930} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5308bb-1224-54c9-a249-060024986a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430934} {:answer/id #uuid "fa91438f-d562-5478-84cb-9cc0479a64c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430930} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f84ddbb-0ce0-5549-8bb9-1dfef0b987f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ce23f40-f256-54cd-9b13-888ae9d3dff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162605" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(y+{z}^{2})\\,i-x\\,j+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot [{e}^{z}]_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\cdot 2\\cdot ({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28fa7678-f13a-5850-9ebd-3577ac1a0379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537311} {:answer/id #uuid "ae79307e-0a76-5408-ab09-a497ed81c568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537560} {:answer/id #uuid "b591f2b8-be38-5e17-9192-b6351f2a138c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537579} {:answer/id #uuid "d60c91b7-6822-585c-88c2-65de5a82f0ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537654} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc29b15-264e-5331-b92c-70ab6eedaf01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbc29b15-264e-5331-b92c-70ab6eedaf01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537669} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b6833fc-e197-5043-abac-1f89f7ffca8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46a23183-289b-59ee-a9e5-bbe0aa79406a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162603" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=4,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y\\sqrt{x}\\,i+(z-x)\\,j+{y}^{3}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{x}]_{x=1}^{x=4}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57dc0f26-61e8-54f6-8765-f4f9dd4d0d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537408} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2113a2-dd4d-539c-984a-b4939769ed41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537521} {:answer/id #uuid "c491e7f1-cd3a-54c5-a9fb-12f4835abac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537606} {:answer/id #uuid "e023c4cd-5ab4-51c0-8881-2596a66eaf02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537680} {:answer/id #uuid "e98c24e5-1c22-5064-93fa-bfdbc05fdb81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a2113a2-dd4d-539c-984a-b4939769ed41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537521} :answer-field/id #uuid "69fd017c-8fd8-594c-96db-bb6def0c9f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56f4d79a-05af-516b-82f5-f4f41e31d95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162598" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=\\pi ,$ $z=2,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={z}^{3}\\,i+2xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+0 \\\\  & =-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{4}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=2}^{z=4} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)\\cdot (\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1745c0ec-91b5-533b-a488-e2bea21d90fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560061} {:answer/id #uuid "43db1007-ab9e-5837-ae42-58fe3ae09f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560068} {:answer/id #uuid "83c9bb48-59e4-5ab1-b9c6-4e4c60698276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560086} {:answer/id #uuid "c384aa9d-923b-599e-bc01-7f1f75575e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer